course: dictionary
lesson: 14596
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them show which meaning the writer wants. Plurality is one of those words. Look at the clues, then pick the meaning that fits.
explain: «The candidate won with a plurality of the votes, but not with more than half.» The clue is votes. Here plurality means the most votes for one choice, even if that is not more than half of all votes.
explain: «The library has a plurality of books, from comics to poems.» Now the clue is books and their variety. Here plurality means a large variety of things. For the grammar meaning, ask whether the word is about more than one thing, like «cats» instead of «cat».
fact: Plurality means more than one, a large variety of things, or the most votes in an election.
fact: In an election, a candidate with a plurality has more votes than anyone else, but not necessarily more than half.
fact: «A plurality of opinions» means many different views living side by side.
fact: In grammar, plural means more than one, as in «dogs» rather than «dog».
fact: In first past the post elections, used in the UK, US and Canada, a plurality of votes is enough to win.
exercise: «The candidate won with a plurality of the votes.» What does plurality mean here? | A long line of people waiting to vote | A party that never wins anything | *The most votes for one candidate, even if not more than half | A rule about how old voters must be
exercise: «The school has a plurality of clubs, from chess to dance.» What does plurality mean here? | A very small number of people | A single club only | *A large variety of things | A kind of sport
exercise: Which word is plural, showing more than one? | *cats | the | cat | sit
test: In the vote, the winner had a plurality of votes. Did she have more than half of all the votes? | She had no votes at all | *No, she had the most, but not more than half | She had exactly one vote | Yes, always
test: Which phrase means many different views living side by side? | *a plurality of opinions | a sharp pencil | a single vote | a quiet room
exam: In a first past the post election, can a candidate win with a plurality? | Never, they must always have more than half | Only in a grammar lesson | Only if there are no other candidates | *Yes, if they have the most votes, even if not more than half
